license on your on-prem system.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:08.790
Maybe you're frustrated
with the inability

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.400
to scale storage and compute.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.510
Maybe you're just frustrated
with the inability

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:15.870
to scale them at all.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.310
Maybe your system
works well enough,

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:24.320
but it's not going to keep up
with your forecasted growth

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:26.910
if you're lucky enough
to be in that problem.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.610
Maybe your current system is
just too hard to maintain,

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:34.470
or takes too much work to keep
it fed and watered and humming

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:35.190
along.

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:36.930
Maybe it's just
too dang expensive,

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:38.680
and you're looking
for an option.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:41.010
So now you're faced
with a choice, right?

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.630
You can either move
now or move later.

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:46.170
At some point you're going to
be forced to make some change,

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:48.600
whether it's buying more
of your legacy system

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.940
or thinking about
something else.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:53.940
And so that's what we're
here to talk about.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:55.885
So for those of you
new to BigQuery,

00:03:55.885 --> 00:03:57.510
we'll talk about this
for a little bit.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:59.964
If you've known BigQuery
for a long, long time,

00:03:59.964 --> 00:04:02.130
I'll call you back in, you
can just check your email

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:03.920
for a couple of minutes here.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:09.210
So BigQuery is Google Cloud's
enterprise data warehouse.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:10.540
It's an analytics tool.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.390
So it's not an OLTP store.

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:15.750
Like Bigtable, we have
other products for that.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.029
This one is focused
on analytics.

00:04:18.029 --> 00:04:23.160
BigQuery goes really
big and really fast.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:26.270
BigQuery, you interact
with BigQuery in SQL.

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:29.550
It's the Lingua Franca
of data analytics.

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:35.080
And so standard SQL is the
coding language that you use.

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:37.800
And because BigQuery
is basically

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:41.430
the public face or
the productization

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:50.110
of a number of the elements
of Google's infrastructure,

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:51.930
our storage is on
the same system

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:56.500
as storage of products
like AdWords, YouTube.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.530
Let's see, another small
one-- oh yeah, Gmail.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:00.155
What's that other
one that Google does?

00:05:00.155 --> 00:05:01.320
Search.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:04.380
So we get a lot of
benefit at BigQuery

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:06.840
by being built on the
exact same infrastructure

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:09.990
that these other, you know,
billion plus user products

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:10.640
utilize.

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:17.440
Now, something that is unique
to BigQuery and not something

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:19.570
that we get from
Google's infrastructure

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:22.120
is that we are fully
managed and serverless.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:24.820
So what that means
is you do not have

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:28.630
to worry about spinning up
reservations, or provisioning,

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:31.180
or figuring out when you're
going to have peak loads.

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:34.330
You just load your data,
write your science,

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:36.880
and then press the red button,
and you execute your queries.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:41.530
Google takes care of managing
all the computation resources

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:42.460
that you need.

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:45.490
And we can scale up
storage almost infinitely.

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:50.200
Our largest customers store
well over 100 petabytes.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:51.040
OK.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:53.310
I'm not going to go through
this slide in detail.

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:56.370
But I do think it's a really
good slide for context.

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:58.800
So Google has an awful
lot of storage options.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:01.050
And if you're in this
migration, like should I migrate

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:02.160
or should I not?

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:06.330
It's important to know what
these other things do, or might

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:07.500
do for you, or might not.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:11.220
So BigQuery is all the way
over there on the left in case

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:13.695
you couldn't find it amidst
all of the other blue hexagons

00:06:13.695 --> 00:06:14.550
here.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.470
And that is the data warehouse.

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:21.210
So it's relational in nature,
but it's not an OLTP system.

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:22.300
It's not a database.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:23.520
It's our data warehouse.

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:27.030
Okeydoke.

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:29.580
So let's talk about
the migration path.

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:32.820
And so here I have the legacy
enterprise data warehouse

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:33.840
on the left.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:35.910
BigQuery there kind
of in the middle.

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:40.370
And then I put this little
chart, this last icon

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:44.220
in to really drive
in the fact that when

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:46.920
you're migrating from
one place to another,

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:48.960
getting the data
landed in BigQuery

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.390
and in the right shape, and
getting the queries to run

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:55.440
appropriately quickly, that's
actually not the end line.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:57.600
The end line is the
understanding and the insight

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:00.212
that you're able to implant
in people's brains, right?

00:07:00.212 --> 00:07:01.920
It's not even the
charts, or whether it's

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:04.620
a bar chart, or a line chart,
or how pretty the colors are.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:06.870
It's the understanding
that people take away.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:10.540
And so I wanted to make that
point explicitly here so

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:14.850
basically that we make sure we
cross all the way to the finish

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:16.140
line.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:19.470
Now, to get data from
a legacy on-prem system

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:23.190
to understanding in
your users' minds,

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:26.160
there are a couple of
steps along the way.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:29.730
Google Cloud Storage is
one such stop for BigQuery.

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:32.100
We're trying to make
this process smoother

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:34.440
and be able to load things
directly into BigQuery.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:36.920
But at the moment, if
you want to migrate

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:39.840
even little amounts of data,
or significant amounts of data,

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:42.910
then doing it through GCS is
the way you're going to do it.

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:45.810
However, we do have
some people, probably

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:48.630
in the room, who make this
much easier by abstracting

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:50.050
that layer out of the way.

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:51.050
And that's our partners.

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:53.258
We'll go through some of
these in detail a little bit

00:07:53.258 --> 00:07:53.820
later on.

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:57.634
But our partner tools will
abstract this step from you.

00:07:57.634 --> 00:07:59.550
And so they'll help you
with schema migration,

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:03.730
they'll help [AUDIO OUT].

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:05.270
Are we good?

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:07.490
They'll help you
with migrations,

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:10.940
data cleanliness,
deduplications, et cetera,

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:14.000
and make it a lot smoother to
get your data into BigQuery.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:19.610
If, however, you want to
control that process in detail,

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:21.170
you can do it all on your own.

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:27.770
Maybe it's more cost
effective for you

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:30.320
to do some
transformations or shift

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:33.169
your schema into the right
format before you move it out.

00:08:33.169 --> 00:08:34.429
That's fine.

00:08:34.429 --> 00:08:38.990
If you're moving data from
the legacy into cloud storage,

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:41.900
you can either use online
transfer, which most people do.

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:45.722
If you don't have really
good internet connections,

00:08:45.722 --> 00:08:47.930
we even offer offline transfer
where you can actually

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:50.390
pull the hard disks out of
your system, put them in a box,

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:53.970
and send them to us, and
we'll load them up for you.

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:56.420
Once you get the
data there, BQ Load

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:58.820
is the command that takes
things from cloud storage

00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:00.410
and drops it into BigQuery.

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:02.270
Now, you have to be
careful here, right?

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:05.272
When you do that you have
to specify the schema.

00:09:05.272 --> 00:09:06.980
And doing it this way
assumes that you've

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:09.200
got the schema
appropriately partitioned

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:11.570
or appropriately designed
inside of cloud storage.

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:14.510
If that's not the case, there
are a number of Google tools

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:18.200
that you can use to
make that happen.

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:22.280
And then second to last
but not least, and Nick

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:26.870
may talk about this,
doing ETL inside of SQL

00:09:26.870 --> 00:09:29.820
is a way to generate a
much more transparent ETL

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:31.970
pipeline for your users.

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:33.950
And last but not least,
you have to make sure

00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:36.850
that you have an efficient
pipeline between BigQuery

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:38.600
and whatever vis tool
you're going to use.

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:41.970
Okeydoke.

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:43.380
We'll spend just
a moment or two,

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:45.399
because I'm running at
the edge of my time.

00:09:45.399 --> 00:09:47.940
We'll spend just a moment or
two talking about the tools that

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:49.080
exist here for you.

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:53.970
So everyone in this room
is on this chart somewhere.

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:57.960
The x-axis is the distance
to Google Cloud Platform.

00:09:57.960 --> 00:09:59.967
And it's virtual
distance, right?

00:09:59.967 --> 00:10:01.925
You're very, very close
