00:11:57.220 --> 00:11:59.220
What I will say is that
depending on the source,

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:03.600
we have partners to help you
ingest data from that source.

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:05.250
Since BigQuery is
a data warehouse,

00:12:05.250 --> 00:12:07.680
I'll just spend
a moment on that.

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:10.740
We've had a lot of success
using partner tools and even

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:14.835
folks managing it themselves
from data sources such as, you

00:12:14.835 --> 00:12:22.280
know, Teradata, Vertica,
Natezza, AWS, Hadoop, and Hive.

00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:23.350
OK.

00:12:23.350 --> 00:12:26.335
So I will now turn it
over Nikhil Mishra, who

00:12:26.335 --> 00:12:28.750
will talk about Yahoo's
experience migrating

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:30.370
an awful lot of
data, to BigQuery.

00:12:33.856 --> 00:12:35.848
[APPLAUSE]

00:12:38.587 --> 00:12:39.832
As you like.

00:12:46.730 --> 00:12:48.690
NIKHIL MISHRA:
I'm Nikhil Mishra.

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:50.870
I'll just walk through
some of the experiences

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:53.030
that we had with BigQuery.

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:55.640
And just probably share
some of the learnings

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:57.530
that we have had as
part of this migration,

00:12:57.530 --> 00:13:02.020
and share as much detail
as I could in this talk.

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:09.759
So I wanted to give
you some context on how

00:13:09.759 --> 00:13:10.800
the Yahoo data pipelines.

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:13.830
And this is a
general model in most

00:13:13.830 --> 00:13:16.690
of the companies where
you are using primarily

00:13:16.690 --> 00:13:21.810
Hadoop for your data processing,
and ETL processing in Hadoop.

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:24.690
So a lot of data gets generated
through our user activity

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:28.190
over the web, on native apps, on
ad servers, and a bunch of data

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:30.540
centers collecting those
data, and moving it out

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:33.484
into an HDFS storage system.

00:13:33.484 --> 00:13:34.650
ETL processes run on Hadoop.

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:37.410
They use MapReduce, Pig,
Hive, whatever you're

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:41.580
comfortable with and whatever
your data transformation needs

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:42.450
are.

00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:44.510
And then you put this
data on Hive, Hive

00:13:44.510 --> 00:13:48.540
on Tez, which is the
fastest possible querying

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:50.110
interface you have there.

00:13:50.110 --> 00:13:53.580
And it goes in the hands
of users through a BI tool.

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:56.610
And that's typically the
pattern that we follow.

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:05.380
What we are trying to do is
not to replace this data flow

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:07.690
with BigQuery or
something on GCP.

00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:14.590
What we are trying to do is
add BigQuery into the mix.

00:14:14.590 --> 00:14:20.090
And I'll talk to you the reasons
for going through this modified

00:14:20.090 --> 00:14:21.300
architecture.

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:23.780
And what the pain points were
with our existing systems,

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:25.850
and what we're
trying to solve here.

00:14:25.850 --> 00:14:28.960
The rest of our system
works pretty well.

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:33.936
And you know, the
goods, as I said,

00:14:33.936 --> 00:14:35.060
the data size is humongous.

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:38.810
Now, I've been advised not
to use the exact numbers

00:14:38.810 --> 00:14:40.280
that we process.

00:14:40.280 --> 00:14:44.300
But I can always say
humongous, freakingly large,

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:45.590
multi-something.

00:14:45.590 --> 00:14:48.834
So I'm going to use
that lingo here.

00:14:48.834 --> 00:14:50.750
So the data size that
we process is humongous.

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:52.730
It's big.

00:14:52.730 --> 00:14:55.520
We do it throughout
the day, every hour.

00:14:55.520 --> 00:14:57.304
The batch system is
extremely stable.

00:14:57.304 --> 00:14:58.220
The pipeline's strong.

00:14:58.220 --> 00:15:00.465
There are massive joins
happening with multiple data

00:15:00.465 --> 00:15:01.160
sets.

00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:05.180
And we've never had any
major issues in our system.

00:15:05.180 --> 00:15:07.620
So this part is
working pretty well.

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:09.570
Now, the pain point,
and this is primarily

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:11.010
from the customer standpoint.

00:15:11.010 --> 00:15:16.900
So once we deliver
the data to our users,

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:18.952
you massage this typically
data transformation.

00:15:18.952 --> 00:15:19.910
We cleaned up the data.

00:15:19.910 --> 00:15:21.470
We standardized it.

00:15:21.470 --> 00:15:24.260
We enriched the data with a
lot of additional annotations.

00:15:24.260 --> 00:15:27.980
We added a lot of
metrics in there

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:29.480
and made it available
for our users.

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:32.397
And they would just go and
query through a BI tool,

00:15:32.397 --> 00:15:33.480
and that's pretty much it.

00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:36.370
That's what you need to
make your data accessible.

00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:40.490
But what is missing
for us was the speed.

00:15:40.490 --> 00:15:43.300
And the speed was
needed at Yahoo scale.

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:48.810
And there are not many
options available.

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:49.940
We tried a lot of things.

00:15:49.940 --> 00:15:51.320
And we keep trying new stuff.

00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:54.650
So every once in a while
we'll just see, you know,

00:15:54.650 --> 00:15:59.370
what eventually turns out
to be the best architecture.

00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:03.330
So the scale at Yahoo, as
I said, is freaking big.

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:07.060
It's multi-petabyte
data warehouse.

00:16:07.060 --> 00:16:09.830
The incremental size, as I said,
our processes run every hour.

00:16:09.830 --> 00:16:11.360
And there is a
reason behind that.

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:12.860
And I'll little bit about that.

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:16.690
And every hour when
we process the data,

00:16:16.690 --> 00:16:19.090
again, it is
multi-terabyte data.

00:16:19.090 --> 00:16:20.140
The concurrence is high.

00:16:20.140 --> 00:16:23.860
And this is the result of
a cultural shift happening

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:26.140
in the company, or has been
going on in the company,

00:16:26.140 --> 00:16:29.410
where we are moving
away from the model

00:16:29.410 --> 00:16:31.480
where they've got a set
of analysts, set of data

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:33.760
engineers, who would
prepare data for you,

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:37.000
massage it for you, build the
dashboards, run your queries,

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:39.289
and hand it over
to you in a platter

00:16:39.289 --> 00:16:41.080
and just explain to
you what exactly it is.

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:43.870
And you make your decisions
on what you wanted to do.

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:46.150
What we are doing is
instead we're just

00:16:46.150 --> 00:16:49.159
handing all the data to
all of our engineers,

00:16:49.159 --> 00:16:50.200
all our product advisors.

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:52.060
No matter who you
are in the company,

00:16:52.060 --> 00:16:53.800
you should have
access to the data.

00:16:53.800 --> 00:16:56.450
And you should be
running your own query.

00:16:56.450 --> 00:16:59.030
Now, when you do that you
need to understand that

00:16:59.030 --> 00:17:00.440
those guys have a daytime job.

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:03.550
They cannot be wrangling
with the data the whole day.

00:17:03.550 --> 00:17:08.172
And one experience
for me was, you know,

00:17:08.172 --> 00:17:10.380
one of our colleagues, a
pretty smart product manager

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:13.490
was talking to him, and
he was using my system,

00:17:13.490 --> 00:17:15.530
and he just ran a query.

00:17:15.530 --> 00:17:17.690
And he said, you know, I'll
go for a smoking break,

00:17:17.690 --> 00:17:20.010
and probably by then
it could be done.

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:21.990
And if you're managing
an analytic system,

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:25.190
that's as humiliating
as it can get.

00:17:25.190 --> 00:17:28.674
If your queries start
taking that much time,

00:17:28.674 --> 00:17:30.590
because that's eating
up a lot of productivity

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:32.890
from your company.

00:17:32.890 --> 00:17:36.850
So key consideration for
us was at this scale that

00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:38.100
are very few options as it is.

00:17:38.100 --> 00:17:40.844
So for us, being
hosted, being managed

00:17:40.844 --> 00:17:43.260
was good, because we didn't
have to worry about setting up

00:17:43.260 --> 00:17:46.170
the system, because that
just adds up to the lead time

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:47.751
before we start testing it out.

00:17:47.751 --> 00:17:49.750
So that's pretty good,
pretty convenient for us.

00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:52.110
SQL, again, it's the
language of choice

00:17:52.110 --> 00:17:53.640
for our data
engineers, analysts,

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:55.350
and pretty much everywhere.

00:17:55.350 --> 00:17:57.840
And with SQL comes BI
integration, pretty good SQL

00:17:57.840 --> 00:17:58.759
integration.

00:17:58.759 --> 00:18:00.300
And one really
important thing for us

00:18:00.300 --> 00:18:03.040
was independent scaling
of compute and storing.

00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:05.304
That was really
important for us.

00:18:05.304 --> 00:18:07.220
Because even though we
have multiple petabytes

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:09.660
of warehouse-- and that's
true for everyone's case--

00:18:09.660 --> 00:18:12.210
even if you have this
multi-petabytes warehouse,

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:13.980
users are not going
to come, and each user

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:17.090
is not going to come and query
all of the data all the time.

00:18:17.090 --> 00:18:19.970
He's going to query a subset
of data, what he needs,

00:18:19.970 --> 00:18:23.010
and probably work with it.

00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:27.780
So we wanted to be able to
adjust our capacity for storage

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:32.650
and compute as we see fit.

00:18:32.650 --> 00:18:34.620
So we set out to test BigQuery.

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:39.560
It fit in most of the
[INAUDIBLE] we laid out.

00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:43.526
And another point we added was
we defined what fast means.

00:18:43.526 --> 00:18:45.150
When we said that we
wanted our queries

00:18:45.150 --> 00:18:48.700
to be interactive and
fast, what fast means here?

00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:50.460
So we said that, you
know, when you're

00:18:50.460 --> 00:18:53.330
running your queries, for
typical day-to-day queries

00:18:53.330 --> 00:18:55.080
that you are running,
daily usage queries,

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:58.581
you know that you're going
to run it every single day.

00:18:58.581 --> 00:19:01.410
We don't want you to
be wasting your time

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:02.432
running those queries.

00:19:02.432 --> 00:19:03.890
We want you to do
your daytime job.

00:19:03.890 --> 00:19:08.250
So we said, you know, 30 seconds
seems to be a good threshold.

00:19:08.250 --> 00:19:10.350
If a user is typically
going through a seven step,

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:13.700
eight step now process to
get answers to his query,

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:16.980
he should be back
probably in a few minutes,

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:19.620
or in a half an hour.

00:19:19.620 --> 00:19:22.170
And we said if you are going
to do your monthly reporting,

00:19:22.170 --> 00:19:23.878
your weekly reporting,
which is typically

00:19:23.878 --> 00:19:27.950
the case with most of
our business users,

00:19:27.950 --> 00:19:29.859
we said less than
a minute should

00:19:29.859 --> 00:19:32.400
be good enough, because that's
something you do once a month,

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:35.720
or a few times in a month.

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:39.250
So with that we started
our process of migration.

00:19:39.250 --> 00:19:41.344
And the very first step
that we do at Yahoo when

00:19:41.344 --> 00:19:42.760
we are doing some
of those things,

00:19:42.760 --> 00:19:44.590
and there's something new--

00:19:44.590 --> 00:19:46.960
as we were moving our
data for the first time,

00:19:46.960 --> 00:19:49.810
or at least what I know
of as the first time,

00:19:49.810 --> 00:19:52.390
outside of our data centers.

00:19:52.390 --> 00:19:54.220
So it ran through a
comprehensive legacy,

00:19:54.220 --> 00:19:56.237
you know, legal review.

00:19:56.237 --> 00:19:57.820
The data that we are
moving should not

00:19:57.820 --> 00:19:59.662
be violating any
policies, any agreements

00:19:59.662 --> 00:20:00.620
we have with our users.

00:20:00.620 --> 00:20:02.920
And that's an important
consideration.

00:20:02.920 --> 00:20:05.170
Another important consideration
was network bandwidth,

00:20:05.170 --> 00:20:09.010
because we were going to move
many, many petabytes of data

00:20:09.010 --> 00:20:10.400
across the wire.

00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:13.690
So we had to be very
clear how we phase it out,

00:20:13.690 --> 00:20:18.174
and if we have enough
bandwidth to support that.

00:20:18.174 --> 00:20:19.590
Another interesting
consideration,

00:20:19.590 --> 00:20:22.140
mostly for engineers
on the team,

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:25.260
was that as we move the data
between the two systems--

00:20:25.260 --> 00:20:27.390
because we already
had our data prepared.

00:20:27.390 --> 00:20:29.850
All we were doing is moving
it from one database,

00:20:29.850 --> 00:20:31.260
one system to another.

00:20:31.260 --> 00:20:35.190
And the data-- we wanted
that the schema also

00:20:35.190 --> 00:20:39.840
moved along with the data.

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:42.910
And partitioning was important
for a couple of reasons.

00:20:42.910 --> 00:20:44.430
We wanted to manage
the retention,

00:20:44.430 --> 00:20:46.390
because as we load the
data into the warehouse,

00:20:46.390 --> 00:20:49.860
over a period of time the
warehouse size increases.

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:52.220
You don't need all the
history in some cases.

00:20:52.220 --> 00:20:53.910
So you should be
able to manage it.

00:20:53.910 --> 00:20:56.555
And you should also be able to
optimize your queries leverage

00:20:56.555 --> 00:20:57.471
and partition pruning.

00:21:00.707 --> 00:21:03.040
One interesting thing we
learned very early in our cycle

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:06.910
was that even though
BigQuery stores

00:21:06.910 --> 00:21:09.400
data in an uncompressed
format, there is no need

00:21:09.400 --> 00:21:13.120
to move your data uncompressed.

00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:15.250
You should be able
to move your data,

00:21:15.250 --> 00:21:18.400
and you should be moving
it, in a compressed format.

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:21.520
And we realized that
BigQuery supports Avro.

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:23.350
GCP supports Avro natively.

00:21:23.350 --> 00:21:25.700
And what we wanted to do is
we wanted to leverage that.

00:21:25.700 --> 00:21:27.040
And we tested it out.

00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:31.030
And it turns out that using
the Avro with [INAUDIBLE],

00:21:31.030 --> 00:21:33.187
we ended up reducing
our size by 6x.

00:21:33.187 --> 00:21:34.520
And that was a pretty good deal.

00:21:34.520 --> 00:21:37.072
So we said, you know, that's
what we're going to use.

00:21:37.072 --> 00:21:38.530
And the schema
migration, which was

00:21:38.530 --> 00:21:43.090
an interesting criteria for
us, and a needed criteria,

00:21:43.090 --> 00:21:43.910
came for free.

00:21:43.910 --> 00:21:46.899
So with Avro, with every single
row, you get your schema.

00:21:46.899 --> 00:21:49.190
And we don't have to worry
about as the schema changes.

00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:52.560
It gets propagated in both the
systems as the ETL progresses.

00:21:55.560 --> 00:21:57.500
Now, we moved the data.

00:21:57.500 --> 00:21:59.999
We moved it in Avro
format, loaded in BigQuery.

00:21:59.999 --> 00:22:01.040
The loads were efficient.

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:03.357
We monitored the load
processes, and we benchmarked

00:22:03.357 --> 00:22:04.190
some of our queries.

00:22:04.190 --> 00:22:05.950
We benchmarked
some of our loads.

00:22:05.950 --> 00:22:10.191
And we kept it within the SLA
when the data was available.

00:22:10.191 --> 00:22:11.690
And one interesting
thing we learned

00:22:11.690 --> 00:22:16.670
was that in order to utilize
the capacity efficiently and not

00:22:16.670 --> 00:22:19.230
to have this blip
every day, where you're

00:22:19.230 --> 00:22:21.920
using the entire
capacity in a daily batch

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:24.380
by moving your
hundreds of terabytes,

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:26.930
or multi-terabytes
of data in one day.

00:22:26.930 --> 00:22:30.140
If you split it out
throughout the day every hour,

00:22:30.140 --> 00:22:32.900
you would be not only speeding
up all the load process,

00:22:32.900 --> 00:22:35.990
you'd be also
needing less capacity

00:22:35.990 --> 00:22:38.030
throughout the day instead
of massive capacity

00:22:38.030 --> 00:22:39.230
once every day.

00:22:39.230 --> 00:22:41.407
And you know, that helps a lot.

00:22:41.407 --> 00:22:42.740
That keeps your pipeline stable.

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:48.727
And that helps you leverage your
capacity much more efficiently.

00:22:48.727 --> 00:22:50.185
Query benchmarking,
what we did, we

00:22:50.185 --> 00:22:53.160
set out 30 second as
our daily, 30 seconds

00:22:53.160 --> 00:22:54.636
in one minute intervals.

00:22:54.636 --> 00:22:56.010
And we were
pleasantly surprised.

00:22:56.010 --> 00:22:58.230
The query response time
was pretty amazing.

00:22:58.230 --> 00:23:01.170
It was way, way below than
what we set out to achieve for.

00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:03.004
So it was a big win.

00:23:03.004 --> 00:23:05.420
Interesting thing we learned
was there is something called

00:23:05.420 --> 00:23:08.030
Coalesce that BigQuery does.

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:09.327
We don't know much about it.

00:23:09.327 --> 00:23:10.910
We typically work
with the Google team

00:23:10.910 --> 00:23:13.100
to make sure that all of
our tables are coalesced.

00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:15.600
And there is a huge difference
between the query performance

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:17.837
when the tables are coalesced
versus they are not.

00:23:17.837 --> 00:23:19.420
You don't have any
visibility into it.

00:23:19.420 --> 00:23:20.930
Typically you work with
your Google partners

00:23:20.930 --> 00:23:23.221
to make sure that [INAUDIBLE]
that is a query problems,

00:23:23.221 --> 00:23:25.550
just talk to them and
make sure that, you know,

00:23:25.550 --> 00:23:28.450
your tables are being coalesced.

00:23:28.450 --> 00:23:29.950
Another thing we
learned was purging

00:23:29.950 --> 00:23:31.630
was a new feature
when we started off.

00:23:31.630 --> 00:23:34.990
And it was an important feature.

00:23:34.990 --> 00:23:37.060
And it's a pseudo column
called partitioned time.

00:23:37.060 --> 00:23:39.250
And you pick your date
to be partitioned,

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:41.170
which is your data
date typically.

00:23:41.170 --> 00:23:44.470
And what you need to make
sure that your users end up

00:23:44.470 --> 00:23:46.060
accessing-- if you
can obscure it,

00:23:46.060 --> 00:23:48.430
if you can have your BI
tool translate partition

00:23:48.430 --> 00:23:51.300
time into data and give
that to your users to query,

00:23:51.300 --> 00:23:53.640
you would be able to easily
partition from your data,

00:23:53.640 --> 00:23:56.260
scan less data, make
it less expensive,

00:23:56.260 --> 00:23:58.720
make it faster for your users.

00:23:58.720 --> 00:24:01.390
What we also did when
we launched BigQuery

00:24:01.390 --> 00:24:04.240
with our customers
was we gave them--

00:24:04.240 --> 00:24:06.145
we are trying out
another hosted system,

00:24:06.145 --> 00:24:07.770
which is something,
again, that I'm not

00:24:07.770 --> 00:24:09.580
going to talk much about--

00:24:09.580 --> 00:24:13.270
and we gave a set of users
the new back-end, but exactly

00:24:13.270 --> 00:24:16.700
the same user interface they
were using with the old system.

00:24:16.700 --> 00:24:18.940
And we kept both the
systems running in parallel.

00:24:18.940 --> 00:24:21.610
And we started monitoring
their usage pattern,

00:24:21.610 --> 00:24:23.215
the query average,
the query response

00:24:23.215 --> 00:24:24.340
time, and all those things.

00:24:24.340 --> 00:24:26.890
And we benchmarked both
the systems side by side.

00:24:26.890 --> 00:24:32.290
And BigQuery was like way
better than the other one

00:24:32.290 --> 00:24:33.130
in all parameters.

00:24:33.130 --> 00:24:35.980
Its speed, its stability,
its query performance.

00:24:35.980 --> 00:24:37.779
Everything else was pretty good.

00:24:37.779 --> 00:24:40.320
And the users didn't even know
that the back-end had changed.

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:42.250
So kind of they were in dark.

00:24:42.250 --> 00:24:44.710
So what that allowed us to do
was over the weekend we just

00:24:44.710 --> 00:24:45.370
flipped the switch.

00:24:45.370 --> 00:24:46.270
We initially
thought that we were

00:24:46.270 --> 00:24:48.144
going to keep running
the systems in parallel

00:24:48.144 --> 00:24:50.570
for a few months.

00:24:50.570 --> 00:24:52.540
But we flipped the
switch in a few weeks,

00:24:52.540 --> 00:24:55.160
because we thought, you know,
it's working out pretty well.

00:24:55.160 --> 00:25:00.270
And we started moving all
our users and our use cases

00:25:00.270 --> 00:25:02.730
over to BigQuery.

00:25:02.730 --> 00:25:05.540
And again, the most
important part here

00:25:05.540 --> 00:25:07.760
is when you're doing this
scale of data migration,

00:25:07.760 --> 00:25:10.670
you would be working pretty
closely with your Google team,

00:25:10.670 --> 00:25:13.170
because there are a lot
of things that happen.

00:25:13.170 --> 00:25:15.280
It's a black box, and
I like it that way.

00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:16.310
You don't want to
worry about what's

00:25:16.310 --> 00:25:17.230
happening behind the scenes.

00:25:17.230 --> 00:25:19.760
If you run into a problem, you
just tell Google, go fix it.

00:25:19.760 --> 00:25:22.760
And they are usually
pretty prompt in fixing it

00:25:22.760 --> 00:25:24.560
instead of you trying
to figure it out

00:25:24.560 --> 00:25:27.390
through the documentation
what's going on.

00:25:27.390 --> 00:25:30.500
So we'll talk a bit more
later on in the panel,

00:25:30.500 --> 00:25:33.710
but it was a pretty
interesting experience

00:25:33.710 --> 00:25:35.630
moving this scale of data.

00:25:35.630 --> 00:25:39.700
And some of the key learnings
I'll share with you.

00:25:39.700 --> 00:25:44.080
Next up, I guess, it's Nick
from "The New York Times."

00:25:44.080 --> 00:25:46.000
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:50.800 --> 00:25:53.020
NICK URSA: Hi, everybody.

00:25:53.020 --> 00:25:53.960
My name is Nick.

00:25:53.960 --> 00:25:56.360
I work as a data scientist
and data engineer

00:25:56.360 --> 00:25:57.800
at "The New York Times."

00:25:57.800 --> 00:26:01.100
I'm actually going to
interrupt my normally scheduled

00:26:01.100 --> 00:26:03.590
performance to tell you about
a little piece of history

00:26:03.590 --> 00:26:05.900
I think we just witnessed.

00:26:05.900 --> 00:26:08.420
In 2006, Doug Cutting
was working at Yahoo.

00:26:08.420 --> 00:26:10.730
And if you don't know
Doug, he was who came up

00:26:10.730 --> 00:26:12.650
with the Hadoop elephant.

00:26:12.650 --> 00:26:14.360
It was his son's plush toy.

00:26:14.360 --> 00:26:16.640
And a lot of Hadoop,
to which many of us

00:26:16.640 --> 00:26:19.740
owe some part of our careers,
was really born at Yahoo.

00:26:19.740 --> 00:26:25.130
So it's kind of historic that
Yahoo has kind of merged back

00:26:25.130 --> 00:26:27.620
using elements of the
original Google File System

00:26:27.620 --> 00:26:30.500
under BigQuery, and they were
the developers of the Yahoo

00:26:30.500 --> 00:26:31.310
that we all used.

00:26:31.310 --> 00:26:34.940
There's kind of this family
reunion that you just saw.

00:26:34.940 --> 00:26:40.880
Anyways, I want to
talk to you about what

00:26:40.880 --> 00:26:43.400
you're interested in
your 20s and your 30s.

00:26:43.400 --> 00:26:50.470
A lot of us kind of grew
up getting interested

00:26:50.470 --> 00:26:52.090
in the technology behind data.

00:26:52.090 --> 00:26:53.830
And we loved scale.

00:26:53.830 --> 00:26:56.745
And we loved getting
things to work.

00:26:56.745 --> 00:26:58.120
But when I got a
little bit older

00:26:58.120 --> 00:26:59.869
and started kind of
working with the data,

00:26:59.869 --> 00:27:02.770
instead of getting the
data to work at all,

00:27:02.770 --> 00:27:04.960
I saw a lot of people
who were disenfranchised

00:27:04.960 --> 00:27:07.000
by the kind of
problems that you're

00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:10.690
having when you're working
with technologies like Hadoop.

00:27:10.690 --> 00:27:13.960
What I realized is that a lot of
people are left out in the cold

00:27:13.960 --> 00:27:17.050
as soon as they hit like a
Java stack trace or something.

00:27:17.050 --> 00:27:20.080
And it led me to switch kind
of from doing data science

00:27:20.080 --> 00:27:21.977
to trying to go back
to data engineering

00:27:21.977 --> 00:27:24.560
at "The New York Times," because
I saw this problem around us.

00:27:24.560 --> 00:27:29.710
We had a situation where
we had a lot of people

00:27:29.710 --> 00:27:31.810
staring at Java stack
traces when they should

00:27:31.810 --> 00:27:34.240
be kind of computing things.

00:27:34.240 --> 00:27:36.677
So it caused us to take
a step back and look,

00:27:36.677 --> 00:27:39.010
not at the technologies that
were available at the time,

00:27:39.010 --> 00:27:41.830
but the technologies that
maybe were nascent at the time.

00:27:41.830 --> 00:27:43.150
And this was last year.

00:27:43.150 --> 00:27:45.820
So to the people in
this room who maybe

00:27:45.820 --> 00:27:47.020
were flirting with the idea.

00:27:47.020 --> 00:27:49.217
You're running Hadoop
stacks, and you're not sure

00:27:49.217 --> 00:27:51.550
if you really want to take a
chance on this thing, which

00:27:51.550 --> 00:27:55.020
is, after all, as a
commercial product, quite new.

00:27:55.020 --> 00:27:58.300
I was exactly where you are last
year at last year's Google Next

00:27:58.300 --> 00:28:00.630
wondering if I'm going
to be risking anything.

00:28:00.630 --> 00:28:04.390
So this is potentially
you one year later.

00:28:04.390 --> 00:28:07.960
At "The New York Times," you
know, we're an older company.

00:28:07.960 --> 00:28:11.410
I think our founding
predates the Civil War.

00:28:11.410 --> 00:28:13.870
And we had a similar
archaeological cross-section

00:28:13.870 --> 00:28:15.160
of data storage technologies.

00:28:15.160 --> 00:28:17.590
We still have a few
mainframes, and they work--

00:28:17.590 --> 00:28:19.780
much respect to mainframes.

00:28:19.780 --> 00:28:22.180
There are just so bulletproof
after all these years,

00:28:22.180 --> 00:28:24.760
but we've got to move
a lot of these things

00:28:24.760 --> 00:28:26.222
into a common place.

00:28:26.222 --> 00:28:28.180
Because if you're going
to work in media today,

00:28:28.180 --> 00:28:30.160
you've got to be able
to know all the touch

00:28:30.160 --> 00:28:32.560
points for a given
person in the data silos

00:28:32.560 --> 00:28:34.390
that have been
created over time.

00:28:34.390 --> 00:28:36.520
Different IT systems for
different departments,

00:28:36.520 --> 00:28:38.205
you know, that's
not going to work

00:28:38.205 --> 00:28:43.570
when you need to respond in 10
milliseconds to a web request.

00:28:43.570 --> 00:28:47.350
So if you can see, we've gone
through Oracle data warehouses.

00:28:47.350 --> 00:28:52.030
We've spun up at least 500
MySQL instances on Amazon.

00:28:52.030 --> 00:28:55.540
Redshift, which I
like, but-- and I'll

00:28:55.540 --> 00:28:56.890
tell you the but later.

00:28:56.890 --> 00:28:59.470
And not maybe a year ago, we
spent-- well, maybe a year

00:28:59.470 --> 00:29:01.900
and a half ago-- $1 million
on our hardware Hadoop cluster

00:29:01.900 --> 00:29:04.180
to cut down all
these cloud costs.

00:29:04.180 --> 00:29:05.740
And I got to tell
you, after we like

00:29:05.740 --> 00:29:08.020
kicked the tires on
Hadoop, it was a no-brainer

00:29:08.020 --> 00:29:11.620
to junk all that capital
and just go for this system.

00:29:11.620 --> 00:29:13.930
So we did that with
that kind of outlay.

00:29:16.790 --> 00:29:19.550
You have people trying to
answer a simple question

00:29:19.550 --> 00:29:21.050
about the business
that we're in,

00:29:21.050 --> 00:29:22.664
and they get this kind of thing.

00:29:22.664 --> 00:29:24.080
And what happened
is that the kind

00:29:24.080 --> 00:29:25.760
of people who can
deal with this problem

00:29:25.760 --> 00:29:30.320
are not the people who know what
our quarterly earnings should

00:29:30.320 --> 00:29:31.085
be.

00:29:31.085 --> 00:29:33.290
Is the business metrics
at the right level?

00:29:33.290 --> 00:29:35.930
And we separated the people who
have the technical capability

00:29:35.930 --> 00:29:37.679
to answer the question
from the people who

00:29:37.679 --> 00:29:39.050
originated the question.

00:29:39.050 --> 00:29:41.760
I think that's one of the
unfortunate sides of big data.

00:29:41.760 --> 00:29:47.690
And so what we want to do is
really bring the capability

00:29:47.690 --> 00:29:49.400
to the outer part of the circle.

00:29:49.400 --> 00:29:51.560
People who are closer
to the product design,

00:29:51.560 --> 00:29:55.280
people who are closer to the
heartbeat of the business.

00:29:55.280 --> 00:29:57.554
We fetishized data
engineering a little bit,

00:29:57.554 --> 00:29:58.970
and it's been fun
for a lot of it.

00:29:58.970 --> 00:30:01.094
But I think we need to make
these tools as polished

00:30:01.094 --> 00:30:03.380
as the tools that we used
to use, as polished as those

00:30:03.380 --> 00:30:06.330
old mainframes, you know?

00:30:06.330 --> 00:30:08.584
And there's a problem
with opensource, I think,

00:30:08.584 --> 00:30:10.250
because the kind of
thing that takes you

00:30:10.250 --> 00:30:12.980
to the 100%, the
polish, is really

00:30:12.980 --> 00:30:16.550
boring QA work of edge
cases and corner cases.

00:30:16.550 --> 00:30:18.260
And from what I
know of engineers,

00:30:18.260 --> 00:30:21.152
they want to solve scaling
problems and new core engines.

00:30:21.152 --> 00:30:22.610
Unfortunately,
we've got businesses

00:30:22.610 --> 00:30:24.230
to run in the meantime.

00:30:24.230 --> 00:30:27.260
So we took a look at kind
of what our priorities were.

00:30:27.260 --> 00:30:29.742
And although machine learning
gets a lot of headlines,

00:30:29.742 --> 00:30:31.700
and certainly everything
in there is important,

00:30:31.700 --> 00:30:33.680
it relies on stable
infrastructure.

00:30:33.680 --> 00:30:35.630
Nothing works unless
you can count on it

00:30:35.630 --> 00:30:37.760
to work without a smoke break.

00:30:37.760 --> 00:30:38.957
We need it to be in SQL.

00:30:38.957 --> 00:30:40.790
We don't want to make
people write Java just

00:30:40.790 --> 00:30:42.470
to answer some question.

00:30:42.470 --> 00:30:43.970
And then once you
do that, you can

00:30:43.970 --> 00:30:47.120
start taking control of the
probably octopus of ways

00:30:47.120 --> 00:30:49.040
you're storing data
in your company.

00:30:49.040 --> 00:30:52.880
And then you can have regular
normal people ask questions.

00:30:52.880 --> 00:30:56.030
Now you have four
times as many people

00:30:56.030 --> 00:30:58.070
getting the answers they want.

00:30:58.070 --> 00:31:01.190
So I'm going to briefly show
you how we conducted our trial.

00:31:01.190 --> 00:31:02.330
We benchmarked performance.

00:31:02.330 --> 00:31:04.070
We took our six
most common queries.

00:31:04.070 --> 00:31:06.410
We estimated price by
looking at the Hive logs

00:31:06.410 --> 00:31:08.660
and seeing what was the data
footprint on each of them

00:31:08.660 --> 00:31:09.980
and adding it up.

00:31:09.980 --> 00:31:11.570
My estimate was that
we were actually

00:31:11.570 --> 00:31:16.045
going to be at 1/3 the costs
of our Redshift cluster.

00:31:16.045 --> 00:31:18.170
It turned out to be 1/4
when we actually put it in.

00:31:18.170 --> 00:31:20.253
And people are running
their queries a lot faster.

00:31:25.080 --> 00:31:26.600
So then we went to
an internal beta.

00:31:26.600 --> 00:31:28.520
And here's a little bit
of social engineering

00:31:28.520 --> 00:31:29.811
I'll talk to on the next slide.

00:31:29.811 --> 00:31:32.110
It's really important
if you do the shift you

00:31:32.110 --> 00:31:34.280
budget for two systems
running at the same time.

00:31:34.280 --> 00:31:35.870
Because this is data,
and you have to make sure

00:31:35.870 --> 00:31:36.530
it is correct.

00:31:36.530 --> 00:31:39.890
So that means you need
an audit benchmark.

00:31:39.890 --> 00:31:41.330
So we kept--

00:31:41.330 --> 00:31:44.250
I'll show you this
a little in zoom.

00:31:44.250 --> 00:31:46.440
So the bottom is our old flow.

00:31:46.440 --> 00:31:49.290
The top is what
we set up for GCP.

00:31:49.290 --> 00:31:52.350
So we split our thing
going to a pub/sub.

00:31:52.350 --> 00:31:55.260
The pub/sub calls the AWS SNS.

00:31:55.260 --> 00:31:57.164
And that goes into dataflow.

00:31:57.164 --> 00:31:58.830
And now, you don't
have to do data flow.

00:31:58.830 --> 00:32:00.960
We wanted to move from
mini-batch systems

00:32:00.960 --> 00:32:02.010
to near real-time.

00:32:02.010 --> 00:32:05.010
And it took three
weeks in the POC

00:32:05.010 --> 00:32:08.490
for us to get latency from
45 minutes, log files, gzip--

00:32:08.490 --> 00:32:10.610
I know some of you
still do this--

00:32:10.610 --> 00:32:11.385
to 45 seconds.

00:32:11.385 --> 00:32:13.260
From the time that you
click to "The New York

00:32:13.260 --> 00:32:15.930
Times," to the time that I
can see your click in a query

00:32:15.930 --> 00:32:19.640
is down to 45 seconds.

00:32:19.640 --> 00:32:22.040
And internally you
get a lot of love.

00:32:22.040 --> 00:32:23.831
No one likes queries to fail.

00:32:23.831 --> 00:32:26.330
But this is the kind of thing
we saw pretty well immediately

00:32:26.330 --> 00:32:27.610
with the beta group.

00:32:27.610 --> 00:32:30.380
And once the beta group started
getting this everyone else

00:32:30.380 --> 00:32:33.830
started getting a
little jealous of it.

00:32:33.830 --> 00:32:35.900
And this is just
organizational information.

00:32:35.900 --> 00:32:38.690
That made the rollout across
the organization really good,

00:32:38.690 --> 00:32:40.850
because everyone's like,
oh, can I try that?

00:32:40.850 --> 00:32:42.714
Oh, can I try that?

00:32:42.714 --> 00:32:44.630
And through that we
learned a little something

00:32:44.630 --> 00:32:47.217
about how to onboard
people to new technology.

00:32:47.217 --> 00:32:48.800
It's really important,
if you do this,

00:32:48.800 --> 00:32:50.450
you write your internal
getting started

00:32:50.450 --> 00:32:52.972
guide that uses your
queries and your data.

00:32:52.972 --> 00:32:54.430
Because if you hit
the Google Docs,

00:32:54.430 --> 00:32:57.700
their first day may
be one of frustration.

00:32:57.700 --> 00:32:59.480
Then we made sure
we had like a chat

00:32:59.480 --> 00:33:01.310
group internal to the
company so that you

00:33:01.310 --> 00:33:05.330
could get the early
users helping each other.

00:33:05.330 --> 00:33:08.150
They became your local heroes.

00:33:08.150 --> 00:33:09.710
And it formed kind
of a Hive mind

00:33:09.710 --> 00:33:11.587
when it came to solving queries.

00:33:11.587 --> 00:33:13.670
And we tried to notice the
little things that were

00:33:13.670 --> 00:33:15.590
keeping people from working.

00:33:15.590 --> 00:33:17.030
There were a few
wins with Google

00:33:17.030 --> 00:33:18.590
here that we didn't expect.

00:33:18.590 --> 00:33:20.660
At first I thought
not having JDBC/ODBC

00:33:20.660 --> 00:33:22.190
was going to be a problem.

00:33:22.190 --> 00:33:23.840
Turns out that
having a web client

00:33:23.840 --> 00:33:26.604
was a bigger advantage, because
it enabled so much casual use.

00:33:26.604 --> 00:33:28.520
When you can give someone
an URL and say, hey,

00:33:28.520 --> 00:33:30.350
check out this
query, versus having

00:33:30.350 --> 00:33:32.270
to get them to set up
a whole kind of client,

00:33:32.270 --> 00:33:35.540
and like download the
opensource JDBC driver,

00:33:35.540 --> 00:33:37.990
it was such a winner to
have the web interface.

00:33:37.990 --> 00:33:40.190
Email account creds,
it helps a lot.

00:33:40.190 --> 00:33:42.470
You don't have to set up all
kinds of LDAP things here

00:33:42.470 --> 00:33:45.350
and there and make it
permissions in the database.

00:33:45.350 --> 00:33:50.510
There is a module in Pandas
called, I think, from GBQ,

00:33:50.510 --> 00:33:53.180
or something like that, which
goes straight to Pandas data

00:33:53.180 --> 00:33:53.680
frame.

00:33:53.680 --> 00:33:56.370
So all the data scientists
were absolutely thrilled.

00:33:56.370 --> 00:33:57.320
And because of the
permissioning you

00:33:57.320 --> 00:33:59.445
can actually use the Google
Groups as mailing lists

00:33:59.445 --> 00:34:01.475
to alert people
of schema changes.

00:34:01.475 --> 00:34:02.975
The first thing
you're going to have

00:34:02.975 --> 00:34:06.020
to do when you start trying
to get useful stuff in there

00:34:06.020 --> 00:34:08.630
is get some of your relational
data in there as well.

00:34:08.630 --> 00:34:11.330
The click stream I showed
you, we did with data flow.

00:34:11.330 --> 00:34:15.214
Ironically, the large data
is the easy stuff to do.

00:34:15.214 --> 00:34:17.630
It's the thousands of tables
all through your organization

00:34:17.630 --> 00:34:20.179
that you're going to finally
do this data lake on that end

00:34:20.179 --> 00:34:23.030
up being kind of the real pain.

00:34:23.030 --> 00:34:25.719
But I don't know, BigQuery--

00:34:25.719 --> 00:34:28.580
all Google Cloud is
Google commercializing

00:34:28.580 --> 00:34:30.440
the technology that
allowed their developers

00:34:30.440 --> 00:34:31.552
to be successful.

00:34:31.552 --> 00:34:33.260
So what you're seeing
is a productization

00:34:33.260 --> 00:34:34.718
of what has made
Google successful.

00:34:34.718 --> 00:34:36.710
That's what we're all
trying to tap into here.

00:34:36.710 --> 00:34:39.120
And they know that when a
Google Developer sits down

00:34:39.120 --> 00:34:40.400
and they're paying them
what they're paying them,

00:34:40.400 --> 00:34:42.358
they want it to be
productive on the first day.

00:34:42.358 --> 00:34:45.050
And I got to say, when you start
loading data into BigQuery,

00:34:45.050 --> 00:34:50.120
you go hit your database,
push it to a FIFO or a file,

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:53.420
and push it into BigQuery,
it works like right away.

00:34:53.420 --> 00:34:56.630
And you know, this thing,
there's no indexes.

00:34:56.630 --> 00:34:58.276
There's no sort order.

00:34:58.276 --> 00:35:00.150
At first I thought,
well, what are the wastes

00:35:00.150 --> 00:35:01.316
to get all this performance?

00:35:01.316 --> 00:35:03.830
But the ability for
me to tell an analyst

00:35:03.830 --> 00:35:05.660
that they can upload
data without worrying

00:35:05.660 --> 00:35:07.580
about the layered
on disk or anything

00:35:07.580 --> 00:35:09.809
like that, it's been a huge win.

00:35:09.809 --> 00:35:11.600
Because now people can
do these loops where

00:35:11.600 --> 00:35:13.940
they have a question, they
have an external data source,

00:35:13.940 --> 00:35:15.500
they push it up into
the database themselves,

00:35:15.500 --> 00:35:17.360
and they can answer
their own question.

00:35:17.360 --> 00:35:19.068
And so I think what
happened is we really

00:35:19.068 --> 00:35:22.030
changed our performance metrics
from-- how does this query run?

00:35:22.030 --> 00:35:24.650
To-- given a question,
how long does it

00:35:24.650 --> 00:35:28.220
take until a reasonable skilled
person can get an answer?

00:35:28.220 --> 00:35:30.625
And so the less people you
have in their loop the better.

00:35:30.625 --> 00:35:32.250
I'm not going to read
all these slides.

00:35:32.250 --> 00:35:34.340
The slides are more for you
if you check it out later

00:35:34.340 --> 00:35:35.750
and you want to know what
are the best practices

00:35:35.750 --> 00:35:37.260
for every type of data.

00:35:37.260 --> 00:35:40.250
These are the things
that I came up with.

00:35:40.250 --> 00:35:42.950
But I do want to talk about
data flow a little bit.

00:35:42.950 --> 00:35:45.410
If you don't need real-time--
and be honest with yourself

00:35:45.410 --> 00:35:46.130
about it--

00:35:46.130 --> 00:35:46.790
don't do it.

00:35:46.790 --> 00:35:48.927
Little small batches
are totally fine.

00:35:48.927 --> 00:35:50.510
A lot of people say
real-time, they're

00:35:50.510 --> 00:35:52.430
still fine with five minutes.

00:35:52.430 --> 00:35:55.285
Data flow can be a little bit
expensive to use streaming,

00:35:55.285 --> 00:35:57.410
because you're just keeping
these connections alive

00:35:57.410 --> 00:35:58.830
no matter what the
data volume is.

00:36:01.697 --> 00:36:03.030
And here are a few other things.

00:36:03.030 --> 00:36:04.330
Telling people to use BQMate--

00:36:04.330 --> 00:36:06.121
for the first time
people will become aware

00:36:06.121 --> 00:36:08.712
of the cost of
what they're doing,

00:36:08.712 --> 00:36:09.920
which is sometimes a shocker.

00:36:09.920 --> 00:36:12.724
But it teaches them how
to write a better query.

00:36:12.724 --> 00:36:15.140
And you probably need to get
out of the habit of ordering.

00:36:15.140 --> 00:36:17.080
I think relational
databases made us do that.

00:36:17.080 --> 00:36:18.640
But on distributed
systems ordering

00:36:18.640 --> 00:36:20.380
can really screw up
your performance.

00:36:20.380 --> 00:36:22.480
So don't order if
you don't need to.

00:36:22.480 --> 00:36:25.480
Finally, don't think of
BigQuery as a database.

00:36:25.480 --> 00:36:28.030
And it's in this uncanny
valley right now,

00:36:28.030 --> 00:36:30.640
because it almost looks
like a relational database.

00:36:30.640 --> 00:36:32.860
But you can't do a lot
of mutations on it.

00:36:32.860 --> 00:36:34.555
But the storage
is the same price

00:36:34.555 --> 00:36:38.110
as cloud storage, which means
you can store your data as

00:36:38.110 --> 00:36:43.240
cheaply in BigQuery, where you
have query access to it, as you

00:36:43.240 --> 00:36:45.850
can as if it were on disk, you
need to push up like a Hive

00:36:45.850 --> 00:36:47.020
cluster to get at it.

00:36:47.020 --> 00:36:49.880
So you basically have--

00:36:49.880 --> 00:36:53.975
all structured data is just as
happy in BigQuery price-wise,

00:36:53.975 --> 00:36:57.150
op-x-wise, as it is
in any other system.

00:36:57.150 --> 00:37:00.581
So finally, I let's talk
about what your KPIs are.

00:37:00.581 --> 00:37:02.330
When we started this
project we were like,

00:37:02.330 --> 00:37:03.500
what should we target?

00:37:03.500 --> 00:37:04.670
Deprecated systems?

00:37:04.670 --> 00:37:05.420
Tables migrated.

00:37:05.420 --> 00:37:07.490
And I said, no, let's
do a survey of people.

00:37:07.490 --> 00:37:09.620
And we did a survey
before and after BigQuery.

00:37:09.620 --> 00:37:14.150
And we ended up with
3.5 to 4.7 out of 5

00:37:14.150 --> 00:37:15.974
in terms of analyst
satisfaction.

00:37:15.974 --> 00:37:17.890
And around Christmas I
get an email like this.

00:37:20.730 --> 00:37:23.680
So I hope that if you
go down this path--

00:37:23.680 --> 00:37:26.000
and you can grab my email,
or grab anybody here,

00:37:26.000 --> 00:37:28.458
because it's a small enough
community that we can help each

00:37:28.458 --> 00:37:29.440
other a lot--

00:37:29.440 --> 00:37:31.400
that you end up with
an email like this

00:37:31.400 --> 00:37:34.610
when you're into your attempt
at trying a technology,

00:37:34.610 --> 00:37:37.501
which I think really
deserves a break.

00:37:37.501 --> 00:37:38.000
That's it.

00:37:38.000 --> 00:37:39.272
Chad?

00:37:39.272 --> 00:37:41.156
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:45.857 --> 00:37:47.190
CHAD JENNINGS: Thanks very much.

00:37:50.162 --> 00:37:51.870
The last part of the
session here-- we've

00:37:51.870 --> 00:37:54.420
got right on 20 minutes or so.

00:37:54.420 --> 00:37:59.050
I'll invite my co-presenters
up onto the stage.

00:37:59.050 --> 00:38:01.720
So also acknowledging the--

00:38:01.720 --> 00:38:04.770
Nick, please-- and
acknowledging the dual purpose

00:38:04.770 --> 00:38:08.100
of these slides, we're going
to talk a little bit right now

00:38:08.100 --> 00:38:11.100
about why bother do
this crazy migration

00:38:11.100 --> 00:38:14.209
thing if it has risks,
and it has work to do,

00:38:14.209 --> 00:38:15.250
and et cetera, et cetera.

00:38:15.250 --> 00:38:17.310
So we'll talk a little
bit about that starting

00:38:17.310 --> 00:38:19.290
with Jason in just a second.

00:38:19.290 --> 00:38:21.990
But I also wanted to
point out that in the deck

00:38:21.990 --> 00:38:23.460
the gentlemen here
were kind enough

00:38:23.460 --> 00:38:27.810
to add some bullet points
about what the benefits were.

00:38:27.810 --> 00:38:30.460
So like you can get the
deck, you can get the video,

00:38:30.460 --> 00:38:34.500
and have a look through these.

00:38:34.500 --> 00:38:35.040
Oh, yeah.

00:38:35.040 --> 00:38:36.060
Sorry.

00:38:36.060 --> 00:38:37.860
This is also for
reference of the related--

00:38:37.860 --> 00:38:38.860
oh, there's the clicker.

00:38:38.860 --> 00:38:39.360
Thanks.

00:38:42.060 --> 00:38:44.190
Some other sessions that
may be interesting to you.

00:38:44.190 --> 00:38:48.060
And then because I chose the
slide with the animation--

00:38:48.060 --> 00:38:50.640
yeah, I won't leave
this one up forever.

00:38:50.640 --> 00:38:53.140
But if you want to
grab our handles,

00:38:53.140 --> 00:38:54.972
you can get a hold
of us another way.

00:38:54.972 --> 00:38:56.930
I'll leave this one up
for a couple of minutes.

00:38:56.930 --> 00:38:59.370
But I wanted to start off
the conversation here.

00:38:59.370 --> 00:39:03.135
And, Jason, ask
you to lead us off.

00:39:03.135 --> 00:39:05.510
What was the benefit of doing
the migration that you did,

00:39:05.510 --> 00:39:07.410
and can you describe
what the migration was?

00:39:07.410 --> 00:39:08.326
JASON JHO: Yeah, sure.

00:39:08.326 --> 00:39:12.710
So I guess I'll start with
just first introducing myself.

00:39:12.710 --> 00:39:16.200
So for those of you who may
not know about Blue Apron,

00:39:16.200 --> 00:39:19.290
we're a company whose mission
is to bring incredible home

00:39:19.290 --> 00:39:21.490
cooking to everybody.

00:39:21.490 --> 00:39:24.400
And in the process of doing
so build a better food system,

00:39:24.400 --> 00:39:28.170
which is essentially how food
is grown and distributed.

00:39:28.170 --> 00:39:32.760
So we're not actually as
big as Yahoo and perhaps

00:39:32.760 --> 00:39:36.030
"The New York Times," in terms
of data, size, and scale.

00:39:36.030 --> 00:39:39.620
But we actually do have a
lot of complex data sets--

00:39:39.620 --> 00:39:41.940
everything from our
warehouses, the physical kind,

00:39:41.940 --> 00:39:44.640
where we actually ship
boxes out to our customers.

00:39:44.640 --> 00:39:47.490
To the consumer website
that we have collecting data

00:39:47.490 --> 00:39:51.060
about consumer behavior,
to all the data

00:39:51.060 --> 00:39:54.245
that is relevant to
shipping and logistics.

00:39:54.245 --> 00:39:55.620
So a lot of
different fragmented,

00:39:55.620 --> 00:39:58.860
heterogeneous data sets
that are relatively complex.

00:39:58.860 --> 00:40:03.330
We rely on that data
to perform analytics

00:40:03.330 --> 00:40:06.600
as well as to help
us forecast demand.

00:40:06.600 --> 00:40:10.860
And to also drive personalized
content to the end user.

00:40:10.860 --> 00:40:14.360
So data is very important
for our business success.

00:40:14.360 --> 00:40:17.640
BigQuery has been an
incredibly important asset

00:40:17.640 --> 00:40:21.390
to our platform and pipeline.

00:40:21.390 --> 00:40:26.670
So one of the things that
really shines about BigQuery,

00:40:26.670 --> 00:40:27.940
it's really simple.

00:40:27.940 --> 00:40:30.930
It's our ability to not
think about performance--

00:40:30.930 --> 00:40:33.990
so tuning query performance,
thinking about indexes

00:40:33.990 --> 00:40:35.430
and capacity planning.

00:40:35.430 --> 00:40:37.710
All of that sort of
magically went away.

00:40:37.710 --> 00:40:41.521
And we instead took that time
to think about the data model.

00:40:41.521 --> 00:40:43.020
What is the right
data model that we

00:40:43.020 --> 00:40:47.910
want to curate for our analytics
and machine learning teams,

00:40:47.910 --> 00:40:52.740
so that they don't have to run
all of these complex processes

00:40:52.740 --> 00:40:55.440
to get data from
different places?

00:40:55.440 --> 00:40:58.320
That semantic layer
that we're building

00:40:58.320 --> 00:41:01.170
can be consistently
shared across the company

00:41:01.170 --> 00:41:03.630
and scale as a business grows.

00:41:03.630 --> 00:41:06.810
And BigQuery is a real
incredible enabler

00:41:06.810 --> 00:41:11.310
for that sort of process, which
leads me to my next point.

00:41:11.310 --> 00:41:16.380
I think having that sort of
scalable petabyte scale data

00:41:16.380 --> 00:41:19.440
warehouse at your fingertips,
without having to really think

00:41:19.440 --> 00:41:23.130
about the management
part of it, has

00:41:23.130 --> 00:41:26.630
allowed our analysts
to really explore data

00:41:26.630 --> 00:41:27.720
at interactive scale.

00:41:27.720 --> 00:41:30.300
And I want to
emphasize interactive.

00:41:30.300 --> 00:41:32.760
I think for the very
first time using Looker,

00:41:32.760 --> 00:41:36.040
which is a tool
that we use for BI,

00:41:36.040 --> 00:41:39.120
we've been able to answer
questions as they come.

00:41:39.120 --> 00:41:41.040
You know, like, the
ability to do full table

00:41:41.040 --> 00:41:44.510
scans, to aggregate
data on demand

00:41:44.510 --> 00:41:47.250
is an incredibly
game changing thing.

00:41:47.250 --> 00:41:50.250
Before we would have
wasted hours and perhaps,

00:41:50.250 --> 00:41:54.780
embarrassingly, days to get
answers to our questions

00:41:54.780 --> 00:41:55.860
that we had.

00:41:55.860 --> 00:42:00.750
So BigQuery, again, has
been a tremendous tool

00:42:00.750 --> 00:42:02.100
to our tool box.

00:42:02.100 --> 00:42:04.710
And finally, I would say
that, to Nick's point

00:42:04.710 --> 00:42:12.060
earlier about storage costs,
having data in BigQuery is--

00:42:12.060 --> 00:42:14.080
number one, it's available
for hot querying.

00:42:14.080 --> 00:42:16.980
But you can actually have large
amounts of historical data

00:42:16.980 --> 00:42:18.120
just sitting there.

00:42:18.120 --> 00:42:20.460
And that's sort of
what we've adopted

00:42:20.460 --> 00:42:25.380
in terms of getting historical
data into the data warehouse

00:42:25.380 --> 00:42:27.730
without having actual
time series data.

00:42:27.730 --> 00:42:30.720
So we've been able to batch
load consistent snapshots

00:42:30.720 --> 00:42:34.770
of our data into BigQuery
using day partitioning

00:42:34.770 --> 00:42:39.000
and essentially have the
ability to query over time.

00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:41.430
So that's been a huge
boost in our ability

00:42:41.430 --> 00:42:45.170
to understand customer
behavior over time.

00:42:45.170 --> 00:42:49.610
So all in all BigQuery has
been an incredible tool.

00:42:49.610 --> 00:42:51.250
And it's allowed
us to stay ahead

00:42:51.250 --> 00:42:54.406
of the insights instead of
playing catch up all the time.

00:42:54.406 --> 00:42:55.280
CHAD JENNINGS: Great.

00:42:55.280 --> 00:42:55.780
Thank you.

00:42:55.780 --> 00:42:58.705
Thank you very much for that.

00:42:58.705 --> 00:43:01.460
I'll say that the team
will be particularly

00:43:01.460 --> 00:43:03.374
pleased to hear that story.

00:43:03.374 --> 00:43:04.790
Actually it was
at this conference

00:43:04.790 --> 00:43:06.920
last year that we introduced
long term storage.

00:43:06.920 --> 00:43:09.860
Where if your table hasn't
been modified for 90 days,

00:43:09.860 --> 00:43:12.800
we just reduce the
price of storage by 50%.

00:43:12.800 --> 00:43:14.840
And it was exactly
to induce people

00:43:14.840 --> 00:43:16.340
to say, hey, let's
keep this around

00:43:16.340 --> 00:43:18.560
and continue to get
value out of it.

00:43:18.560 --> 00:43:21.170
So that's terrific.

00:43:21.170 --> 00:43:26.240
I wanted to start out with a
quick question here for you.

00:43:26.240 --> 00:43:28.130
How did you guys,
when you were first

00:43:28.130 --> 00:43:30.770
adopting BigQuery, or
first thinking about it,

00:43:30.770 --> 00:43:32.790
how did you design
the proof of concept?

00:43:32.790 --> 00:43:34.457
JASON JHO: You want
me to take this one?

00:43:34.457 --> 00:43:36.414
CHAD JENNINGS: Yeah, why
don't you lead us off?

00:43:36.414 --> 00:43:37.630
JASON JHO: Sure.

00:43:37.630 --> 00:43:39.050
For us it was really simple.

00:43:39.050 --> 00:43:41.410
To Nick's point, getting
started with BigQuery

00:43:41.410 --> 00:43:44.652
is as easy as opening up
a URL on your web page

00:43:44.652 --> 00:43:45.610
and putting in a query.

00:43:45.610 --> 00:43:48.940
So we actually took a couple
of the most expensive queries

00:43:48.940 --> 00:43:53.080
that we have, queries that
would actually run for hours,

00:43:53.080 --> 00:43:55.180
and without much
migration work at all

00:43:55.180 --> 00:43:57.850
we demonstrated that this thing
can run in less than a couple

00:43:57.850 --> 00:43:58.880
of minutes.

00:43:58.880 --> 00:44:03.430
So that was a huge just proof
of concept right off the bat.

00:44:03.430 --> 00:44:05.482
So yeah, it was really
as simple as that.

00:44:05.482 --> 00:44:06.940
CHAD JENNINGS: And,
Nikhil, you had

00:44:06.940 --> 00:44:10.960
a very different, like orders
of magnitude, proof of concept

00:44:10.960 --> 00:44:11.890
to design.

00:44:11.890 --> 00:44:14.260
How did you pick that?

00:44:14.260 --> 00:44:16.672
How did you design it?

00:44:16.672 --> 00:44:20.150
NIKHIL MISHRA: So for us, yes,
it took us time to get started.

00:44:20.150 --> 00:44:23.890
So we had to think
through a lot of stuff.

00:44:23.890 --> 00:44:26.530
And the way we
decided was we didn't

00:44:26.530 --> 00:44:33.610
want to go to a specific query
model, or benchmark something--

00:44:33.610 --> 00:44:36.670
that's industry
standard benchmark--

00:44:36.670 --> 00:44:38.050
or one of those things.

00:44:38.050 --> 00:44:40.760
We essentially wanted to
replicate a real life use case.

00:44:40.760 --> 00:44:43.610
What that required was to
actually move our real data out

00:44:43.610 --> 00:44:44.110
there.

00:44:44.110 --> 00:44:46.600
And that requires all the kind
of prep that I talked about.

00:44:46.600 --> 00:44:48.730
Go through the legal review.

00:44:48.730 --> 00:44:50.100
That's a very important step.

00:44:50.100 --> 00:44:53.452
And we started off with that.

00:44:53.452 --> 00:44:54.910
The network bandwidth
was something

00:44:54.910 --> 00:44:58.000
that we actually did not start
off looking at initially when

00:44:58.000 --> 00:45:00.180
we did it.

00:45:00.180 --> 00:45:04.180
We actually ended up choking our
pipe between Yahoo and Google

00:45:04.180 --> 00:45:06.460
by moving a massive
amount of data.

00:45:06.460 --> 00:45:08.590
We brought down some of
our internal systems.

00:45:08.590 --> 00:45:12.662
And that's where
the consideration

00:45:12.662 --> 00:45:14.620
has become so important
for everybody at Yahoo.

00:45:14.620 --> 00:45:15.680
And it was something new.

00:45:15.680 --> 00:45:19.210
And we assumed a lot of stuff.

00:45:19.210 --> 00:45:20.020
And we were wrong.

00:45:20.020 --> 00:45:23.910
And as we started off
migrating the data,

00:45:23.910 --> 00:45:25.420
we also were not aware--

00:45:25.420 --> 00:45:27.360
and I believe Avro
is still beta.

00:45:27.360 --> 00:45:28.202
I don't know.

00:45:28.202 --> 00:45:29.660
But at that point
we were not aware

00:45:29.660 --> 00:45:34.760
that Avro is natively supported,
which is a huge deal for us.

00:45:34.760 --> 00:45:38.020
So when we got those pieces
together what we did was

00:45:38.020 --> 00:45:41.020
we really picked up
our real life use case.

00:45:41.020 --> 00:45:44.000
And we were using another
third party hosted cloud.

00:45:44.000 --> 00:45:46.400
And we had something
going on there.

00:45:46.400 --> 00:45:49.520
And we just picked it
up and moved it here

00:45:49.520 --> 00:45:51.050
and moved some
additional data sets

00:45:51.050 --> 00:45:55.240
to just try out Google's
claim of petabyte scale.

00:45:55.240 --> 00:45:57.470
And it worked out pretty well.

00:45:57.470 --> 00:45:59.650
And we replicated the use case.

00:45:59.650 --> 00:46:01.600
And as I said, without
telling our users

00:46:01.600 --> 00:46:02.770
we flipped the switch.

00:46:02.770 --> 00:46:05.880
They didn't even know that they
were querying another database.

00:46:05.880 --> 00:46:07.860
We used Looker as our BI tool.

00:46:07.860 --> 00:46:11.260
And Looker had pretty
interesting integration

00:46:11.260 --> 00:46:14.030
with legacy SQL.

00:46:14.030 --> 00:46:16.700
The legacy Google SQL,
which is not on single plan.

00:46:16.700 --> 00:46:19.360
Which I love, by the way,
it is pretty interesting.

00:46:19.360 --> 00:46:21.770
It's fun to use.

00:46:21.770 --> 00:46:22.840
So we just hooked it up.

00:46:22.840 --> 00:46:27.010
We created exactly-- all we had
to do was flip the connection,

00:46:27.010 --> 00:46:29.210
reuse the same
model, same stuff,

00:46:29.210 --> 00:46:31.510
hand it over to set up
users, and just monitor them.

00:46:31.510 --> 00:46:32.470
And that's what we did.

00:46:32.470 --> 00:46:34.720
And that allowed us, and
that gave us the confidence

00:46:34.720 --> 00:46:36.910
that this is something that
we could possibly work with.

00:46:36.910 --> 00:46:39.118
CHAD JENNINGS: Yeah, you
were in a fortunate position

00:46:39.118 --> 00:46:41.050
where you had a cloud
workflow running,

00:46:41.050 --> 00:46:43.145
and you were able to
switch that one over and do

00:46:43.145 --> 00:46:45.220
a direct compare.

00:46:45.220 --> 00:46:49.240
I wanted to ask Nick
a question here.

00:46:49.240 --> 00:46:52.570
So BigQuery, if you're thinking
about just doing lift and shift

00:46:52.570 --> 00:46:56.530
from a Teradata instance
or something like that,

00:46:56.530 --> 00:47:01.340
BigQuery is not optimized
for a star schema, right?

00:47:01.340 --> 00:47:03.010
BigQuery wants the
tables flattened.

00:47:03.010 --> 00:47:05.440
So there are definitely some--
let me not sugarcoat that.

00:47:08.530 --> 00:47:11.710
There was a session yesterday
on appropriate data models,

00:47:11.710 --> 00:47:14.230
so we're not going to go
into that in detail here.

00:47:14.230 --> 00:47:15.910
But I did want to ask Nick--

00:47:15.910 --> 00:47:19.520
when you do that shift there's
an opportunity for you to,

00:47:19.520 --> 00:47:23.755
A, endure some heartache and
do some work, like redesign--

00:47:23.755 --> 00:47:26.752
NICK URSA: We did,
and it was heartache.

00:47:26.752 --> 00:47:28.210
CHAD JENNINGS: So
the question here

00:47:28.210 --> 00:47:31.960
is, how much schema
migration do you want to do?

00:47:31.960 --> 00:47:35.317
The analogy is when you do
remodeling at your home,

00:47:35.317 --> 00:47:37.650
you pull the plasterboard off
and you're like, oh, geez.

00:47:37.650 --> 00:47:39.760
Do I do the electrical as well?

00:47:39.760 --> 00:47:40.990
So how far do you take that?

00:47:43.856 --> 00:47:45.730
NICK URSA: I don't want
to say that you can't

00:47:45.730 --> 00:47:49.120
do star schema in BigQuery,
especially if your dimension

00:47:49.120 --> 00:47:50.232
tables are small.

00:47:50.232 --> 00:47:51.190
They're going to like--

00:47:51.190 --> 00:47:53.230
CHAD JENNINGS: You can,
it's just not optimal.

00:47:53.230 --> 00:47:53.938
NICK URSA: Right.

00:47:53.938 --> 00:47:55.240
Right.

00:47:55.240 --> 00:47:56.890
Well, the thing is--

00:47:56.890 --> 00:47:59.180
maybe other places have
this, too, where there's

00:47:59.180 --> 00:48:00.550
a clickstream data flow.

00:48:00.550 --> 00:48:04.420
And clickstream dataflows
are high volume, wide tables.

00:48:04.420 --> 00:48:07.030
Often you're only taking
a subset of the data drop.

00:48:07.030 --> 00:48:10.090
And then you have like a
bunch of data warehouses

00:48:10.090 --> 00:48:11.470
with smaller tables.

00:48:11.470 --> 00:48:14.020
And we're at the point
that those two worlds

00:48:14.020 --> 00:48:16.540
are merging inside BigQuery.

00:48:16.540 --> 00:48:19.540
So we used to do a lot of
stamping in applied values,

00:48:19.540 --> 00:48:23.170
because we knew we
couldn't join at it later.

00:48:23.170 --> 00:48:26.710
I think that we were a little
more conservative in BigQuery,

00:48:26.710 --> 00:48:28.450
because we saw that
we could actually

00:48:28.450 --> 00:48:31.340
get good join performance in
some of the smaller tables.

00:48:31.340 --> 00:48:33.280
But if anything, we
had to scale back

00:48:33.280 --> 00:48:34.900
on how much we denormalized.

00:48:34.900 --> 00:48:38.080
We normalized as if we
were going to run on Hadoop

00:48:38.080 --> 00:48:40.150
and never do a
join in the world.

00:48:40.150 --> 00:48:43.030
And we found BigQuery actually
had better join performance

00:48:43.030 --> 00:48:46.180
coming from a Hadoop workflow.

00:48:46.180 --> 00:48:48.580
But when you revisit
things it's a chance

00:48:48.580 --> 00:48:52.540
to do those things that you
kind of never had the chance to.

00:48:52.540 --> 00:48:56.710
So we did, I think, more
importantly in the performance

00:48:56.710 --> 00:48:59.290
was just a data
governance exercise

00:48:59.290 --> 00:49:02.170
of-- what are these fields?

00:49:02.170 --> 00:49:03.520
Are we still using them?

00:49:03.520 --> 00:49:05.410
Do they mean what
they think they mean?

00:49:05.410 --> 00:49:09.130
And although it delayed
our rollout by a month,

00:49:09.130 --> 00:49:12.640
it's important that everybody
who sees a column name

00:49:12.640 --> 00:49:16.000
and makes a logical assumption
of what that thing means--

00:49:16.000 --> 00:49:17.110
that they're right.

00:49:17.110 --> 00:49:19.750
Because I think worse than slow
queries, worse than anything

00:49:19.750 --> 00:49:21.970
is getting two
different answers.

00:49:21.970 --> 00:49:24.180
So I think it's more
of the data governance

00:49:24.180 --> 00:49:26.620
that drove the schema
redesign than any performance

00:49:26.620 --> 00:49:27.730
consideration.

00:49:27.730 --> 00:49:29.410
CHAD JENNINGS: Interesting.

00:49:29.410 --> 00:49:31.280
I'll also invite
the audience here.

00:49:31.280 --> 00:49:33.160
So I have a couple
more questions,

00:49:33.160 --> 00:49:35.434
but I'm going to run out
before the session is over.

00:49:35.434 --> 00:49:36.850
So I'd like to
invite the audience

00:49:36.850 --> 00:49:40.150
to come up and ask
questions of us as well.

00:49:40.150 --> 00:49:44.590
Please, use the microphone
so that it's on the record.

00:49:44.590 --> 00:49:47.530
I'll give you a second.

00:49:47.530 --> 00:49:51.580
I did want to ask Nikhil,
what's been the biggest

00:49:51.580 --> 00:49:55.290
impact of migrating to
BigQuery and having BigQuery

00:49:55.290 --> 00:49:59.365
be that like fast store at
the end of your block diagram?

00:50:02.835 --> 00:50:06.020
NIKHIL MISHRA:
It's productivity.

00:50:06.020 --> 00:50:09.740
There was one of the PMs in
Yahoo, a pretty smart guy,

00:50:09.740 --> 00:50:12.950
he actually put a sheet together
and handed it over to me

00:50:12.950 --> 00:50:15.490
after we did this migration.

00:50:15.490 --> 00:50:18.140
And he had a dollar
amount of how much

00:50:18.140 --> 00:50:21.020
we saved improving
the productivity

00:50:21.020 --> 00:50:22.270
of different product managers.

00:50:22.270 --> 00:50:25.120
And he took himself as a base,
which is not the right thing

00:50:25.120 --> 00:50:29.490
to do, because he did all
kinds of crazy steps there.

00:50:29.490 --> 00:50:32.950
But I think that
to me is a big win.

00:50:32.950 --> 00:50:37.344
Initially, even though we had
all the data easily available

00:50:37.344 --> 00:50:38.760
and accessible to
the users, users

00:50:38.760 --> 00:50:42.460
knew that that's where the
data is, and I can query it,

00:50:42.460 --> 00:50:43.210
there was a fear.

00:50:43.210 --> 00:50:44.509
There was a barrier.

00:50:44.509 --> 00:50:46.800
And the barrier was they
didn't know whether they would

00:50:46.800 --> 00:50:48.310
run the query and
whether they'll

00:50:48.310 --> 00:50:50.110
be able to get the
answer back, right?

00:50:50.110 --> 00:50:54.110
Depending on how much
data they're querying.

00:50:54.110 --> 00:50:56.900
Now, what's the load on the
system at that point in time?

00:50:56.900 --> 00:51:02.620
And by nature, you know, it was
more suited for batch loads.

00:51:02.620 --> 00:51:07.510
And it kind of, you know,
with concurrent users,

00:51:07.510 --> 00:51:10.240
we just ended up waiting way too
long than we would anticipate

00:51:10.240 --> 00:51:11.920
when a single user
or a few set of users

00:51:11.920 --> 00:51:13.600
are running the queries.

00:51:13.600 --> 00:51:14.800
So I think that was a big.

00:51:14.800 --> 00:51:19.150
When users don't think
twice before just logging

00:51:19.150 --> 00:51:21.220
into the system and
running their queries,

00:51:21.220 --> 00:51:22.300
that's a huge win.

00:51:22.300 --> 00:51:24.214
CHAD JENNINGS: Fantastic.

00:51:24.214 --> 00:51:25.880
NICK URSA: I just
wanted to add to that.

00:51:25.880 --> 00:51:28.780
Which is that it's not
only an efficiency thing,

00:51:28.780 --> 00:51:30.310
it's an innovation thing.

00:51:30.310 --> 00:51:33.294
Because you know what it's
like when you try something out

00:51:33.294 --> 00:51:34.210
and it's five minutes.

00:51:34.210 --> 00:51:35.710
You lose your train of thought.

00:51:35.710 --> 00:51:38.395
Imagine if your IDE didn't
respond in five minutes.

00:51:38.395 --> 00:51:40.270
But that's what it's
like for an analyst when

00:51:40.270 --> 00:51:41.680
they run in BigQuery.

00:51:41.680 --> 00:51:43.930
And so when you're trying
to refine a business model

00:51:43.930 --> 00:51:46.900
or you're trying to search
for the cause of a problem,

00:51:46.900 --> 00:51:48.790
the ability to
retain your train of

00:51:48.790 --> 00:51:51.540
thought as you query
is hugely valuable.

00:51:51.540 --> 00:51:53.530
And people like
Justin are literally

00:51:53.530 --> 00:51:55.794
having so much more fun now.

00:51:55.794 --> 00:51:57.460
CHAD JENNINGS: That's
a wonderful story.

00:51:57.460 --> 00:51:57.959
Yes, sir.

00:51:57.959 --> 00:51:59.090
Please.

00:51:59.090 --> 00:52:02.350
AUDIENCE: I wanted to ask you,
do you have any scenario where

00:52:02.350 --> 00:52:06.070
the data need to be updated,
and how frequent are

00:52:06.070 --> 00:52:07.450
the data need to be updated?

00:52:07.450 --> 00:52:09.070
How you solve it?

00:52:09.070 --> 00:52:12.400
And does that affect
your schema design?

00:52:15.365 --> 00:52:16.990
NICK URSA: I mean,
I might want to talk

00:52:16.990 --> 00:52:18.280
to you about what it is.

00:52:18.280 --> 00:52:21.100
When it comes to, like, logs
and aggregations, append only,

00:52:21.100 --> 00:52:24.280
obviously those don't
need to be updated.

00:52:24.280 --> 00:52:25.870
So let's look at the categories.

00:52:25.870 --> 00:52:27.610
Large things that
need to be updated,

00:52:27.610 --> 00:52:29.237
small things that
need to be updated,

00:52:29.237 --> 00:52:31.320
small things that don't
need to be updated-- well,

00:52:31.320 --> 00:52:32.590
let's not even talk about that--

00:52:32.590 --> 00:52:34.548
and large things that
don't need to be updated.

00:52:34.548 --> 00:52:36.430
So the last category
is very easy.

00:52:36.430 --> 00:52:37.829
Those are just append only.

00:52:37.829 --> 00:52:39.370
Small things that
need to be updated,

00:52:39.370 --> 00:52:40.953
we have relational
databases for that.

00:52:40.953 --> 00:52:43.624
So for me, the solution
to that is just replicate,

00:52:43.624 --> 00:52:44.505
replicate, replicate.

00:52:44.505 --> 00:52:46.630
And there's a company called
Alooma, which can even

00:52:46.630 --> 00:52:50.320
read my SQL bin log files
directly and move them

00:52:50.320 --> 00:52:51.551
into BigQuery for you.

00:52:51.551 --> 00:52:53.300
And so the tricky one
is the first column.

00:52:53.300 --> 00:52:55.216
Things that need to be
updated that are large.

00:52:55.216 --> 00:52:57.760
This is the use case that
Bigtable was designed for.

00:53:01.067 --> 00:53:02.650
When you're in that
category you maybe

00:53:02.650 --> 00:53:05.140
need to look at different
storage options.

00:53:05.140 --> 00:53:07.510
You know, Spanner may actually
be a good choice for you

00:53:07.510 --> 00:53:08.800
if you have large scale.

00:53:08.800 --> 00:53:13.450
But a lot of people can
get by on a 256 gigabyte

00:53:13.450 --> 00:53:16.540
backed SSD PostgreSQL instance.

00:53:16.540 --> 00:53:18.160
The 96 is amazing.

00:53:18.160 --> 00:53:19.180
SSDs are amazing.

00:53:19.180 --> 00:53:22.620
And a lot of RAM solves
a lot of your problems.

00:53:22.620 --> 00:53:26.560
CHAD JENNINGS: If I may, I'll
just add one thought to that.

00:53:26.560 --> 00:53:28.570
Nick mentioned Bigtable.

00:53:28.570 --> 00:53:30.340
I can't say what it
is now, but there's

00:53:30.340 --> 00:53:33.340
a talk at 5:20 that
has a lot to do

00:53:33.340 --> 00:53:35.950
with how Bigtable and
BigQuery might possibly, maybe

00:53:35.950 --> 00:53:37.000
possibly work together.

00:53:37.000 --> 00:53:38.190
NICK URSA: Hint, hint.

00:53:38.190 --> 00:53:39.523
CHAD JENNINGS: Yeah, no kidding.

00:53:39.523 --> 00:53:41.650
Hint, hint.

00:53:41.650 --> 00:53:45.582
So to take Nick's point, if you
have to do a lot of streaming,

00:53:45.582 --> 00:53:47.290
then you can load it
into BigQuery query,

00:53:47.290 --> 00:53:49.800
and then you can
export it from there.

00:53:49.800 --> 00:53:50.554
5:20.

00:53:50.554 --> 00:53:52.220
JASON JHO: Yeah, so
we actually do both.

00:53:52.220 --> 00:53:54.850
So we take consistent
transactional snapshots

00:53:54.850 --> 00:53:55.960
of our relational data.

00:53:55.960 --> 00:53:59.500
And we batch load them into
BigQuery several times a day.

00:53:59.500 --> 00:54:01.300
But we also have
event data that's

00:54:01.300 --> 00:54:03.880
coming in through it like
an append only fashion,

00:54:03.880 --> 00:54:05.590
streaming that
directly into BigQuery.

00:54:05.590 --> 00:54:07.390
So I think depending
on your needs,

00:54:07.390 --> 00:54:11.440
for us we have the benefit of
taking our relational data,

00:54:11.440 --> 00:54:14.320
sort of loading them
into a big history table.

00:54:14.320 --> 00:54:17.260
But we also have sort of
real-time event data coming in,

00:54:17.260 --> 00:54:17.810
as well.

00:54:17.810 --> 00:54:21.520
So it really serves well
for both of those use cases.

00:54:21.520 --> 00:54:22.540
CHAD JENNINGS: Yes, sir?

00:54:22.540 --> 00:54:28.360
AUDIENCE: My question is
about separation for the data.

00:54:28.360 --> 00:54:31.690
So do you have a roll
based security implemented

00:54:31.690 --> 00:54:34.750
in your systems, guys, somehow?

00:54:34.750 --> 00:54:36.490
CHAD JENNINGS: Yeah, actually.

00:54:36.490 --> 00:54:38.530
Thank you for
bringing this one up.

00:54:38.530 --> 00:54:42.830
So the GCP security model is
based on GCP's IAM-- identity,

00:54:42.830 --> 00:54:44.920
access, and management.

00:54:44.920 --> 00:54:50.920
And so you can get granularity
down to row and column access

00:54:50.920 --> 00:54:53.290
by creating views and then
giving particular sets

00:54:53.290 --> 00:54:57.109
of people access to that view.

00:54:57.109 --> 00:54:57.650
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:54:57.650 --> 00:54:59.820
Thank you.

00:54:59.820 --> 00:55:00.867
CHAD JENNINGS: Yes, sir?

00:55:00.867 --> 00:55:03.075
AUDIENCE: I'm just curious
how you guys control costs

00:55:03.075 --> 00:55:06.120
by like throwing Looker
on top of BigQuery

00:55:06.120 --> 00:55:09.519
and releasing it
out in the wild.

00:55:09.519 --> 00:55:12.060
JASON JHO: I think Looker comes
with a dry run functionality.

00:55:12.060 --> 00:55:15.360
You can actually like
estimate the number of bytes

00:55:15.360 --> 00:55:17.850
scanned since that's how
you actually get charged.

00:55:17.850 --> 00:55:19.270
So that's one thing that we do.

00:55:19.270 --> 00:55:21.310
We also have budget calculators.

00:55:21.310 --> 00:55:24.457
And I think Google actually has
a tool that allows you to cap--

00:55:24.457 --> 00:55:25.290
CHAD JENNINGS: Yeah.

00:55:25.290 --> 00:55:28.650
There's a form that you
can fill out online.

00:55:28.650 --> 00:55:30.039
Apologies, it's a bit clunky.

00:55:30.039 --> 00:55:31.830
But you can fill out
the form and say, hey,

00:55:31.830 --> 00:55:35.820
I want to cap spend on
X project at Y dollars.

00:55:35.820 --> 00:55:37.960
It's basically quota control.

00:55:37.960 --> 00:55:39.120
AUDIENCE: Thanks.

00:55:39.120 --> 00:55:42.140
NICK URSA: By far, the largest
cost is mistaken queries,

00:55:42.140 --> 00:55:45.120
not like the normal ones.

00:55:45.120 --> 00:55:46.650
CHAD JENNINGS: Yes, sir?

00:55:46.650 --> 00:55:48.150
AUDIENCE: First of
all, I appreciate

00:55:48.150 --> 00:55:51.094
you said that migrating
star Schema is not easy.

00:55:51.094 --> 00:55:52.010
You didn't sugar coat.

00:55:52.010 --> 00:55:54.480
So the reality is
that there are a lot

00:55:54.480 --> 00:55:57.200
of legacy star schemas,
Teredata, Netezza, or whatever,

00:55:57.200 --> 00:55:57.870
right?

00:55:57.870 --> 00:55:59.390
I'm just curious
if any of you guys

00:55:59.390 --> 00:56:01.230
had the experience,
how you went about it.

00:56:01.230 --> 00:56:03.240
Did you just try
to break it down?

00:56:03.240 --> 00:56:04.060
Chunk it down?

00:56:04.060 --> 00:56:06.180
What stays as star schema?

00:56:06.180 --> 00:56:09.390
What are some things which
you can refactor, redesign?

00:56:09.390 --> 00:56:11.160
What are some
effective strategies?

00:56:11.160 --> 00:56:12.399
I'm just curious on that.

00:56:12.399 --> 00:56:13.440
CHAD JENNINGS: Thank you.

00:56:13.440 --> 00:56:17.580
JASON JHO: So I might not be
the best person to answer this,

00:56:17.580 --> 00:56:20.520
but we just threw the same
data model into BigQuery

00:56:20.520 --> 00:56:23.100
because it joins.

00:56:23.100 --> 00:56:25.620
So that actually
allowed us to buy time

00:56:25.620 --> 00:56:29.190
to do that, get the benefits
of the query performance,

00:56:29.190 --> 00:56:30.860
and in parallel
think about, well,

00:56:30.860 --> 00:56:32.610
how do I denormalize
and flatten this data

00:56:32.610 --> 00:56:34.680
so that we can really optimize?

00:56:34.680 --> 00:56:37.380
So I think just the
fact that it supports

00:56:37.380 --> 00:56:40.770
standard SQL and big
joins is a huge win,

00:56:40.770 --> 00:56:47.190
because it allows you to
be effective immediately

00:56:47.190 --> 00:56:50.340
and giving you time to really
think about the optimal data

00:56:50.340 --> 00:56:51.217
schema.

00:56:51.217 --> 00:56:53.050
AUDIENCE: Because some
of the other sessions

00:56:53.050 --> 00:56:55.200
they talked about
repeating using

00:56:55.200 --> 00:56:58.080
the nested repeated features,
but that requires a rewrite

00:56:58.080 --> 00:56:59.460
in SQL, to your point.

00:56:59.460 --> 00:57:01.700
You've got to train all
the analysts to un-nest,

00:57:01.700 --> 00:57:03.050
which is an investment.

00:57:03.050 --> 00:57:04.260
That's what happened.

00:57:04.260 --> 00:57:05.093
CHAD JENNINGS: Yeah.

00:57:05.093 --> 00:57:06.830
I might I might add also--

00:57:06.830 --> 00:57:09.060
so the session on
the data models

00:57:09.060 --> 00:57:10.740
is the best place
to go for that.

00:57:10.740 --> 00:57:15.010
The video actually should
be available today.

00:57:15.010 --> 00:57:16.770
But I will say,
in the experience

00:57:16.770 --> 00:57:18.750
that I've had helping
people specifically

00:57:18.750 --> 00:57:23.820
from Teradata migrate, the lift
and shift, with the expectation

00:57:23.820 --> 00:57:25.320
that it's going to
work just like it

00:57:25.320 --> 00:57:28.210
did before is a fool's errand.

00:57:28.210 --> 00:57:31.032
That's just not going to
work, and we found that

00:57:31.032 --> 00:57:31.740
out the hard way.

00:57:31.740 --> 00:57:33.490
Because we tried it
once, and it was like,

00:57:33.490 --> 00:57:35.970
oh, that was not a good idea.

00:57:35.970 --> 00:57:39.000
And so I can't give you
too much advice here

00:57:39.000 --> 00:57:41.850
without like sitting down
with a piece of paper,

00:57:41.850 --> 00:57:44.190
but it definitely
takes some doing.

00:57:44.190 --> 00:57:46.980
We found that the partner tools
have been very useful here.

00:57:46.980 --> 00:57:48.914
Although, partner
tools, it's basically

00:57:48.914 --> 00:57:50.580
like they'll give you
a pair of scissors

00:57:50.580 --> 00:57:53.310
and invite you to go running.

00:57:53.310 --> 00:57:55.980
They're not going to give
you advice about what

00:57:55.980 --> 00:57:57.480
shape to put your data in.

00:57:57.480 --> 00:57:59.800
They're going to let you code
that up any way you want.

00:57:59.800 --> 00:58:01.710
So it's a useful
tool, but you still

00:58:01.710 --> 00:58:03.510
need to do that design work.

00:58:03.510 --> 00:58:05.893
Do reference that other
talk from yesterday.

00:58:05.893 --> 00:58:06.726
AUDIENCE: All right.

00:58:06.726 --> 00:58:07.530
Thank you.

00:58:07.530 --> 00:58:10.347
CHAD JENNINGS: Yes, sir?

00:58:10.347 --> 00:58:11.430
In the blue shirt, please.

00:58:11.430 --> 00:58:12.852
AUDIENCE: Go first.

00:58:12.852 --> 00:58:13.800
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:58:13.800 --> 00:58:16.140
CHAD JENNINGS: All right,
you guys can fight it out.

00:58:16.140 --> 00:58:19.870
AUDIENCE: I was just wondering
if the custom partitioning was

00:58:19.870 --> 00:58:21.390
going to come in
the near future.

00:58:24.630 --> 00:58:26.790
CHAD JENNINGS: So
BigQuery's partitioning

00:58:26.790 --> 00:58:31.050
is not as full featured
as some others.

00:58:31.050 --> 00:58:34.590
And yeah, so custom partitioning
based on any user's hash

00:58:34.590 --> 00:58:36.030
is definitely on our roadmap.

00:58:36.030 --> 00:58:37.860
I really can't speculate.

00:58:37.860 --> 00:58:39.330
I know where it
is on the roadmap.

00:58:39.330 --> 00:58:41.550
I can't share it here
at a public forum.

00:58:41.550 --> 00:58:45.420
But let me say it this way.

00:58:45.420 --> 00:58:48.180
So BigQuery is well aware
that as we market ourselves

00:58:48.180 --> 00:58:52.140
as an enterprise data warehouse,
and as we talk about things

00:58:52.140 --> 00:58:54.050
like Teradata, and
we talk about Oracle,

00:58:54.050 --> 00:58:55.680
and moving from
those things, that we

00:58:55.680 --> 00:58:58.560
need to answer use cases
with features that people

00:58:58.560 --> 00:59:01.200
are accustomed to having.

00:59:01.200 --> 00:59:03.270
And so the team has
that really very much

00:59:03.270 --> 00:59:06.420
in the front of their mind.

00:59:06.420 --> 00:59:08.700
You guys have heard
this story before.

00:59:08.700 --> 00:59:11.220
When we look at features
that we're prioritizing,

00:59:11.220 --> 00:59:12.660
I put them into 2 buckets.

00:59:12.660 --> 00:59:15.390
It's table stakes and
market differentiators.

00:59:15.390 --> 00:59:18.930
And things like hashed
partitioning are table stakes.

00:59:18.930 --> 00:59:24.060
We have to do that
to be really useful.

00:59:24.060 --> 00:59:26.190
And so those things
tend to get prioritized

00:59:26.190 --> 00:59:27.626
when we're doing our work.

00:59:27.626 --> 00:59:28.400
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:59:28.400 --> 00:59:30.200
CHAD JENNINGS: Thank you.

00:59:30.200 --> 00:59:31.320
AUDIENCE: Hello.

00:59:31.320 --> 00:59:31.820
Hi.

00:59:31.820 --> 00:59:34.230
I'm Tiger, coming from Seattle.

00:59:34.230 --> 00:59:35.830
First I want to
say, this session

00:59:35.830 --> 00:59:37.290
is a very solid session.

00:59:37.290 --> 00:59:40.330
It's the best I have ever
taken since yesterday.

00:59:40.330 --> 00:59:42.520
It's full of good practice
and good knowledge.

00:59:42.520 --> 00:59:43.530
Thank you so much.

00:59:43.530 --> 00:59:44.720
CHAD JENNINGS: It's the
best one since yesterday?

00:59:44.720 --> 00:59:45.450
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:59:45.450 --> 00:59:46.840
CHAD JENNINGS: That's awesome.

00:59:46.840 --> 00:59:47.060
[LAUGHING]

00:59:47.060 --> 00:59:48.310
NICK URSA: What was yesterday?

00:59:48.310 --> 00:59:50.940
CHAD JENNINGS: Shh, shh,
just take the compliment.

00:59:50.940 --> 00:59:53.450
AUDIENCE: My question is as
the artificial intelligence

00:59:53.450 --> 00:59:55.440
and machine learning
is in fashion,

00:59:55.440 --> 00:59:57.920
do you guys think you
want to integrate it

00:59:57.920 --> 01:00:00.765
with artificial intelligence
somehow in with your data

01:00:00.765 --> 01:00:03.460
warehouse solution?

01:00:03.460 --> 01:00:05.400
Or any practice already?

01:00:05.400 --> 01:00:06.345
NICK URSA: No.

01:00:06.345 --> 01:00:07.255
[LAUGHING]

01:00:07.255 --> 01:00:08.010
CHAD JENNINGS: OK.

01:00:08.010 --> 01:00:10.000
Give me.

01:00:10.000 --> 01:00:14.479
NICK URSA: So most ML
starts with clean data.

01:00:14.479 --> 01:00:16.270
I guess they can write
clustering functions

01:00:16.270 --> 01:00:18.186
or whatever but the
computational requirements

01:00:18.186 --> 01:00:19.870
of most machine
learning algorithms

01:00:19.870 --> 01:00:26.140
are not appropriate for the
thing that BigQuery's based on.

01:00:26.140 --> 01:00:28.510
BigQuery is based on
being able to subdivide

01:00:28.510 --> 01:00:29.890
your data tremendously.

01:00:29.890 --> 01:00:32.900
But a lot of machine learning
is about global optimums,

01:00:32.900 --> 01:00:36.010
and there's so much
shuffling in between.

01:00:36.010 --> 01:00:37.197
So who's doing this?

01:00:37.197 --> 01:00:37.780
I'll tell you.

01:00:37.780 --> 01:00:42.470
Spotify is now using a lot of
BigQuery as the source data,

01:00:42.470 --> 01:00:45.940
because good ML starts
with clean data.

01:00:45.940 --> 01:00:48.010
And having a nice
repository that you

01:00:48.010 --> 01:00:50.230
can pull training
data from quickly

01:00:50.230 --> 01:00:51.720
and know that it's right--

01:00:51.720 --> 01:00:55.579
and then the algo is
like icing on the cake.

01:00:55.579 --> 01:00:56.412
CHAD JENNINGS: Yeah.

01:00:56.412 --> 01:00:58.930
We're being indicated that
we're out of time here.

01:00:58.930 --> 01:01:00.830
So let me just add
a step to that.

01:01:00.830 --> 01:01:02.830
And if there are folks
with questions remaining,

01:01:02.830 --> 01:01:06.940
I think the speakers will be
available a little bit after.

01:01:06.940 --> 01:01:08.830
But to talk about
integrating BigQuery

01:01:08.830 --> 01:01:11.320
with machine learning,
that is something

01:01:11.320 --> 01:01:15.000
that is very much top of mind
in that market differentiator

01:01:15.000 --> 01:01:16.090
column.

01:01:16.090 --> 01:01:19.860
So it's prioritized often behind
things like hash partitioning.

01:01:19.860 --> 01:01:21.100
But you can use it now.

01:01:21.100 --> 01:01:21.970
It's a bit clunky.

01:01:21.970 --> 01:01:25.880
But you can export data out
of BigQuery, store it as a CSV

01:01:25.880 --> 01:01:27.580
in Google Cloud Storage.

01:01:27.580 --> 01:01:29.720
And then use that
as input to a cloud

01:01:29.720 --> 01:01:31.440
ML call or something like that.

01:01:31.440 --> 01:01:33.430
But we definitely
have at top of mind

01:01:33.430 --> 01:01:36.441
to smooth out that workflow.

01:01:36.441 --> 01:01:36.940
All right.

01:01:36.940 --> 01:01:38.565
Folks, thank you so
much for your time.

01:01:38.565 --> 01:01:39.960
To my co-presenters.

01:01:39.960 --> 01:01:41.160
[APPLAUSE]

01:01:42.060 --> 01:01:44.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

